## Deploying a Static Website on AWS S3

**Live Website:** https://elikplim-reborn.s3.eu-north-1.amazonaws.com/index_files/index.html

### Overview

This project illustrates deploying a static website using Amazon Simple Storage Service (S3), configuring the environment for public access, and resolving associated permission challenges.

### Process

#### Repository Setup
- Initialized the project with an `index.html` file and an `html` folder dedicated to storing images and assets.

#### Bucket Configuration
- Created an S3 bucket to house the website files and enabled static website hosting in the bucket properties.

#### Permission Management
- Configured Access Control Lists (ACLs) and applied a JSON bucket policy to ensure the content could be read publicly.

### Challenges Encountered

#### Access Control Lists (ACLs)
- Initially, the required ACLs were not enabled, resulting in access errors when attempting to view the website.
- Activating ACLs was essential for achieving public visibility of the website content.

#### JSON Bucket Policy
- The JSON bucket policy had to be precisely defined to grant explicit permission for retrieving objects.
- Implementing the correct syntax resolved the final access issues and enabled public read access.

### Bucket Policy Configuration

```json
{
    "Version": "2012-10-17",
    "Statement": [
        {
            "Sid": "PublicReadOnlyAccess",
            "Effect": "Allow",
            "Principal": "*",
            "Action": "s3:GetObject",
            "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::elikplim-reborn/*"
        }
    ]
}
```

This policy grants public read-only access to all objects in the S3 bucket, allowing anyone to retrieve and view the website files.

### Key Learnings

- Proper IAM policy configuration is crucial for S3 static website hosting
- Both ACLs and bucket policies may be required depending on AWS S3 settings
- Wildcard principals (`*`) enable public access but should be used cautiously in production environments
